    "body": "Great on any Friday: Anker USB-A to USB-C Cable - 2-pack, 3.3ft/1m, Great for Android Auto\nDetroit's Automakers Are Being Asked to Build America's Drone Supply Chain\n29 photos\nPhoto: General Motors\nGeneral Motors could soon be making propulsion systems for military drones. The proposed motors would be closer in size to something found in a riding lawn mower than a small block or the Mongoose turbo diesel, making said motors a very different proposition for Detroit's largest automaker.\nPentagon officials, as reported by the Wall Street Journal, are in preliminary discussions with General Motors, the Ford Motor Company, the folks at Magna International, and companies outside the automotive industry. Said talks are centered on supplying vital components, including motors, lidar sensors, actuators, and so forth. Drone companies would remain fully responsible for the production of the aircraft themselves.\nNo agreement has been finalized thus far, with GM declining to comment on the specific proposal. Magna, on the other hand, confirmed preliminary discussions. Ford's involvement may be a little different. Although the Wall Street Journal included the Blue Oval among the companies approached by the Pentagon, it has been revealed that Ford is currently focused on military vehicles and its recent investment in Revolution Space .\nIn other words, ruggedized trucks for the military and the satellite business. Turning our attention back to General Motors, there is a precedent that has to be highlighted. GM's defense division has been working with Lockheed Martin to make housing components for the PAC-3 interceptor missile used by Patriot systems, with PAC-3 being a very expensive surface-to-air design.\nLockheed Martin announced in September 2026 that the first components had been delivered less than a month after the contract was signed, with General Motors bringing its commercial manufacturing capabilities to the defense program. Building production capacity quickly is the name of the game here.\nPhoto: Ford UK / General Dynamics UK Presumably, the Pentagon wants to replicate that performance with its next military drones. General Motors has literal decades of experience with casting, machining, electric propulsion systems, and very complex supply chains. On its own, General Motors can easily coordinate suppliers, purchase industrial quantities of materials, and run production processes at scale.\nMost young drone manufacturers cannot match that, and US-based drone manufacturers are a bit lacking in this regard. The General Atomics MQ-1 Predator pioneered modern unmanned aerial missions in the 1990s, and the MQ-9 Reaper is now the best-known frontline armed drone in US military service. Fewer than 600 units of the MQ-9 Reaper have been produced to date, partly due to its exorbitant price. A combat-configured drone costs more than $30 million.\nMaking a missile housing is a different thing from supplying drone components, though, especially powertrain components. GM could be facing rapidly evolving propulsion designs, changing military requirements, and fierce pressure to keep unit costs low. The Pentagon hasn't made the procurement side of the biz attractive to outside manufacturers either.\nPer the WSJ, six separate efforts were once underway to develop a one-way attack drone. All were pursuing the same goal, yet working to different specifications. That kind of duplication is the stuff of nightmares for suppliers, and looking at the bigger picture, an automaker cannot efficiently invest in a production line when the Pentagon has yet to decide which version of the subject component it wants.\nThe Pentagon estimates the military drone market could reach $20 to $30 billion per annum, all while its Drone Dominance Program has no more than $1.1 billion to produce more than 200,000 one-way attach drones by 2027. The $1.1 billion initiative also covers lethal payloads and modular warheads.\nVia // WSJ\nLiked the article? Follow us to see more stories from autoevolution in your Google feeds.\nGM Ford Magna military drone US\nAbout the author: Mircea Panait\nAfter a 1:43 scale model of a Ferrari 250 GTO sparked Mircea's interest for cars when he was a kid, an early internship at Top Gear sealed his career path. He's most interested in muscle cars and American trucks, but he takes a passing interest in quirky kei cars as well. Full profile\nUP NEXT 1000-HP Ford GT Used in the Gran Turismo Movie Is Up for Grabs",
